---
import LegalLayout from "@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { LINKS } from "@/consts";
---

<LegalLayout
	title="Code signing policy"
	description="How the Bramble desktop app's Windows installer is built and signed with Azure Artifact Signing."
	path="/code-signing"
	meta="Effective date: 10 November 2026"
>
	<h2>What is signed</h2>
	<p>
		The Windows installer for the Bramble desktop app is Authenticode-signed with Microsoft's
		<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/artifact-signing/">Azure Artifact Signing</a>. Microsoft
		verifies who the publisher is before issuing the certificate, and Windows shows that publisher when
		you run the installer. A changed installer no longer matches its signature.
	</p>
	<p>Only Bramble's own software is signed with this certificate.</p>

	<h2>How a signed build is made</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>From the public source, on GitHub's machines.</strong> GitHub Actions builds the installer
			on GitHub-hosted runners, directly from the public
			<a href={LINKS.github}>source repository</a>. Nothing built on a personal computer is signed.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Only from the main branch.</strong> Signing works only for builds of the main branch of
			that repository. No signing key or password is stored anywhere: Azure trusts GitHub's own proof
			of which workflow is asking.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The build scripts are source too.</strong> The workflow that builds and signs the
			installer lives in the repository and goes through the same review as the rest of the code.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Team roles</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Committers and reviewers:</strong>
			<a href="https://github.com/flythenimbus">@flythenimbus</a>. Changes from anyone else arrive
			as pull requests and are reviewed before they are merged.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Release approvers:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/flythenimbus">@flythenimbus</a>,
			who approves each release before it is published.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Everyone in these roles uses multi-factor authentication for GitHub and for Azure.</p>

	<h2>Privacy</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble does not transfer any information to other networked systems unless you ask it to, with
		one exception: the desktop and mobile apps check for updates, which you can turn off. Every
		network connection Bramble can make is described in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Other downloads</h2>
	<p>
		This policy covers the Windows installer. Bramble's other downloads are signed through their own
		channels: the macOS app with Bramble's Apple Developer ID and notarized by Apple, the browser
		extensions and the iOS app by their stores, and the Android and Linux releases with Bramble's own
		release keys.
	</p>

	<h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
	<p>
		If you think a signed Bramble installer is not what it should be, or you find a security issue,
		please report it privately through
		<a href={`${LINKS.github}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub's security advisories</a>.
	</p>
</LegalLayout>
